WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:07.320
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Welcome, everybody, to the
NHS public meeting. I should just start by

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.889
saying that Ara Darzi’s had to give his
apologies. He’s working on this REACT study

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:18.305
at Imperial on Covid. Are there any declarations
of interest?

00:00:18.305 --> 00:00:22.029
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: David, we should probably
just record, I’m not sure it was in the

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:26.160
last board meeting, my appointment to the
interim chair of the National Institute for

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.059
Health Protection.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Dido. I should have

00:00:29.059 --> 00:00:34.790
– yeah. Thank you for that. Can we just
approve the minutes of the last board meeting?

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:41.280
Yes? Thank you. Well, the first item is the
Simon, the chief executive, to give us an

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:48.890
oral report. Simon.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Thank you very much, David,

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:53.940
and afternoon, colleagues. We are obviously
going to be talking specifically this afternoon,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:59.460
amongst other things, about where we stand
on the coronavirus response, but also more

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:05.930
widely, the ramping up of services across
the whole of the NHS.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:10.790
We said at the end of July, when we spoke,
that there was a window of opportunity between

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:15.390
July, August, September, October, before we
head into the winter and concerns about a

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:21.900
possible second Covid waved, in which it would
be possible to fire up all the cylinders across

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:29.120
the health service. And as you hear from Amanda,
that work is progressing well. However, it

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:36.130
is also the case that, as has been now publicly
reported, we are seeing increasing coronavirus

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:45.659
cases in several regions across the country.
And as we speak, we have just over 2000 coronavirus

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:52.890
-positive patients in NHS hospital beds. That
is four times more at the end of September

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:58.030
than at the beginning of September, although
of course that is still thankfully substantially

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:06.730
fewer than the 19,000 peak acute inpatient
beds are – that we had for coronavirus patients

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:15.170
in mid-April. So, across the health service,
detailed work to envisage and to look at escalation

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:22.080
plans against different amounts of coronavirus
pressure. We will, again, discuss that a bit

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:28.020
later this afternoon.
And in addition, we are also gearing up for

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:35.319
the winter season. Flu vaccinations, staff
support that would be happening come what

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:40.920
may. So it is going to be a very pressurised
six months for the health service coming off

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:46.000
the back of what has also been an incredibly
pressurised six months, and as we get into

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.320
the discussion this afternoon, I know that,
as always, we will want to pay particular

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.610
thanks to our frontline staff across the health
service.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:59.410
And in that regard, I think that the last
thing I would just say is that, of course,

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:04.300
this marks the beginning of Black History
Month, and that is always a timely moment

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:10.270
to remember the contribution that Black, Asian,
and minority ethnic members of staff across

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:16.500
the health service have made, not just now,
but right from the get-go. And the Windrush

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:22.680
generation, famously, were part of the generation
who helped nurture and sustain the NHS from

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:30.319
the beginning. Wind the clock forward 70 years,
we can see through the impact of coronavirus,

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:35.370
the differential impact that inequality, including
race-based inequality, is having, not only

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:42.069
across our workforce but across our communities.
So, I think at a future meeting, we will take

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:48.060
the opportunity to hear the work that the
new NHS Race and Health Observatory is embarked

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:55.050
on, together with the wider programme of work
that NHS England and NHS Improvement are leading.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:59.300
I think that’s probably enough just to get
started, David, but as always, happy to take

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.269
questions.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Well, Simon, maybe I could

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:07.760
ask you one question. I mean, if you cast
your mind back to early March, and the state

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.680
that the NHS was in at the time, and sort
of trying compare that with where we are today,

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:18.420
in terms of our readiness to face up to what
may be a second wave of coronavirus, how would

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:22.610
you characterise the NHS’ sort of state
of readiness at the moment?

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:28.719
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, I think there are several
things that are different than the position

00:04:28.719 --> 00:04:35.350
in mid-March. The first, of course, is that
there is more community surveillance and testing,

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:42.139
so that there is a better appreciation of
the extent to which coronavirus is spreading

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:47.949
in the community. And what follows from that
is that there is a better understanding about

00:04:47.949 --> 00:04:53.110
the particular pressures now, in the north-west,
parts of the north-east, and parts of the

00:04:53.110 --> 00:05:01.180
Midlands, some concern growing in north-east
London, whereas, frankly, in February and

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:08.400
early March, the absence of that testing infrastructure
meant that it was not appreciated by the public

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.900
health epidemiologists how much coronavirus
there was in the population. So, I think the

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:17.970
first thing to say is we’ve got a better
surveillance system as a country than we had

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:22.499
six months ago.
The second thing to say is that, in terms

00:05:22.499 --> 00:05:28.939
of the NHS, obviously a lot of the rapid mobilisation
that happened, whether that was in securing

00:05:28.939 --> 00:05:40.409
ventilator supply, creating buffer capacity,
redeploying teams, creating Covid-safe environments

00:05:40.409 --> 00:05:49.060
for care of non-Covid patients, that work
is now well established, and we have available

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:53.339
to us, were there to be a continuing increase
in the second wave.

00:05:53.339 --> 00:06:01.149
And I suppose the third thing to say is that
the clinical insights around effective treatments

00:06:01.149 --> 00:06:07.139
have advanced quite considerably since then,
not least as a result of the randomised controlled

00:06:07.139 --> 00:06:14.289
trials that have been run out of this country,
and which have produced the two first evidence-based

00:06:14.289 --> 00:06:19.520
treatments that reduce mortality for Covid,
and many others in the pipeline as well.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:26.830
So, you know, on those three fronts, both
in terms of surveillance, in terms of capacity,

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:32.580
and in terms of treatments and clinical practice,
we are in a better place. But none of that

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:39.550
should detract from the fact that, were we
to see continuing growth in coronavirus coming

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:46.979
alongside the winter pressures, that would
obviously be very undesirable from a public

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:50.699
health point of view, from the point of view
of excess mortality, and from the point of

00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:55.659
view of impact on other NHS services. So,
it is vitally important that the measures

00:06:55.659 --> 00:07:02.020
that are being set out by government, that’s
the Test and Trace operation that Dido is

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:06.939
leading, you know, continue to play their
part alongside the actions that we, the people

00:07:06.939 --> 00:07:11.649
of this country, ourselves take to reduce
transmission between people.

00:07:11.649 --> 00:07:19.869
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Simon. David Roberts?
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Just to build on that answer,

00:07:19.869 --> 00:07:24.499
Simon, I’d be interested in your – before
we go into the sort of the – the next agenda

00:07:24.499 --> 00:07:30.491
item, I’d be interested in your perspectives
on the state of the workforce, because, for

00:07:30.491 --> 00:07:37.051
all the plans and everything, fundamentally
based on the – on how our people are, and

00:07:37.051 --> 00:07:41.729
they’ve done, as you rightly pointed out,
an incredible job, but we are now facing into

00:07:41.729 --> 00:07:47.050
a number of months of similar challenges.
Hopefully not as significant as last time.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:51.099
And I just be really interested, as you think
about the various sort of levels and layers

00:07:51.099 --> 00:08:00.060
of the – of the NHS family, how – how
well supported and their sort of state of

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:05.419
readiness, if I could use that term, for what
is coming ahead of us. Thank you, David.

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:10.970
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, I think there have
been huge pressures across the health service,

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:16.289
and that has been experienced, you know, very
acutely, obviously in places such as critical

00:08:16.289 --> 00:08:22.939
care, our intensivists, anaesthetists, our
thoracic medicine, many other specialties,

00:08:22.939 --> 00:08:24.749
and lots of other people who have pitched
in.

00:08:24.749 --> 00:08:30.619
But it’s also, frankly, been experienced
in our primary care, in mental health services,

00:08:30.619 --> 00:08:36.210
where we are seeing more people with mental
health needs coming forward, the community

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:40.970
health services are obviously a vital part,
given that the majority of people with coronavirus

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:46.010
aren’t sick enough such that they require
hospitalisation.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:51.140
So, I suppose, a way of responding to what
you are saying, David, is that we went in

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:58.430
in February, March, into the first phase of
the pandemic, with people who were already

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:02.320
under pressure having come out of winter,
but that is doubly so now, because the last

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:08.220
six months have been, you know, highly stressful
for many colleagues. So, there are various

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:12.880
things that we are doing nationally, and working
with employers locally on.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:21.560
Obviously, the health and well-being support
in the workplace is vitally important. Frankly,

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:26.960
ensuring that we’ve got as many people as
we can to help, and that’s not just now,

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:35.660
but it’s a sense that others are going to
join. Ruth might talk about the very substantial

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:39.430
upsurge in interest in joining the nursing
profession that we’ve actually seen on the

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:46.870
part of young people on the back of the last
six months. Applications and acceptances to

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:52.080
undergraduates nursing degrees are, I think,
we are likely to blow the barn doors off.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:57.100
I think we are going to have a very, very
strong intake. So, that is all good news for

00:09:57.100 --> 00:10:02.180
the future, but doesn’t deal with the immediate
pressures of the here and now, but maybe Ruth

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:05.920
and Prerana, if she’s on, would like to
come in as well.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:10.230
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ruth, would you like to comment
from a nursing perspective?

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:14.790
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you very much, chair, and
just to build on what Simon has said, you

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:19.920
know, nurses, midwives, care staff, have worked
extraordinarily hard through this pandemic,

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:25.360
and I’ve been very proud of the way they’ve
stepped forward with their huge clinical expertise

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:28.790
and experience.
One of the things we’ve noticed though is

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:33.440
that, whilst we’ve been able to retain more
of our staff, which has been fantastic, we’ve

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:40.320
been able to recruit more staff, and our vacancies
are coming down each month, our healthcare

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:45.990
assistant vacancies have almost halved, all
that is really important. And of course, we

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:50.080
have started to welcome our international
colleagues coming over, and I’m really pleased

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:53.870
with the work that Duncan has been leading
with that with me.

00:10:53.870 --> 00:11:00.570
But of course, the psychological support that
our staff have needed, not just those in critical

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:05.871
care, but those people that are in theatres,
that went to the aid of critical care, those

00:11:05.871 --> 00:11:12.390
people in mental health organisations, those
people in community, so, working with Prerana,

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:17.570
of course, talking and thinking about what
extra support and psychological support that

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:22.670
we can give our staff.
And as Simon has said, working with Mark Radford

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:30.410
in HEE, we’ve seen a huge increase in the
number of people wanting to become a nurse.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:37.891
If you think about people accessing the interest
in health careers on the internet, 210% increase

00:11:37.891 --> 00:11:44.950
in the searchers on how to become a nurse,
which is fantastic. 43,000 people wanting

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:51.800
and applying to be a nurse. And [inaudible]
been leading the piece of work to not just

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:57.620
focus on the undergraduate, but the postgraduate
people coming into our profession. The nursing

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:03.590
associates converted into registered nursing,
and of course what HEE are doing around the

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:08.780
blended degree. So, I’m hoping that by the
time the figures finally come in, that we’ll

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:15.101
see – well, a massive increase in the number
of people coming into our profession. Maybe

00:12:15.101 --> 00:12:20.000
even the most we’ve seen for over 20 years.
And that would be just a delight.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:26.340
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Ruth. Steve, do
you have a comment from – from the medical

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:30.340
side? The doctors’ side?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yeah. Well, I would really

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:37.330
just emphasise what both Simon and Ruth have
said. Staff clearly did a magnificent job

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:43.050
in the spring and into the summer. Not only
in maintaining and making sure that patients

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:49.050
with Covid were treated, when we had that
peak, but also that they also kept other services

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:57.010
going, cancer services, where 85% of cancer
treatment continued between March and July,

00:12:57.010 --> 00:13:02.250
but they also worked with cancer patients
where it wasn’t the right thing to do to

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:07.650
continue treatment, perhaps patient preference,
so there was a lot going on that wasn’t

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:12.510
Covid during that period as well. More than
200,000 people, for instance, treated for

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:17.300
cancer during the peak of the pandemic.
I’m sure staff are looking at the headlines

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:22.820
in the news, and they will have concerns about
increased numbers of Covid patients, and I’m

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:27.250
sure that there is some trepidation about
going into a second wave. But I’d be absolutely

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:33.860
confident that, as always, our NHS staff will
step up. They care passionately about caring

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:38.620
for patients, Covid patients, and all patients,
and they will do what’s required.

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:44.180
But, as Simon said at the end, this is really
a responsibility for everybody. I said many

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:49.480
times in the spring that it was the efforts
of all of us as citizens, the public, in reducing

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:55.850
our contacts, in maintaining social distancing,
that actually allowed our staff and the NHS

00:13:55.850 --> 00:14:11.510
to do [inaudible] hasn’t changed [inaudible]
our staff [inaudible] think about social distancing

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:18.620
rules, they reduced contacts where they can,
and they really – we all play a part in

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:22.070
stopping this virus spreading.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Ian, I’m going

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:25.279
to ask you to come in, and then I’m going
to ask Emily, just on the procurement side,

00:14:25.279 --> 00:14:29.310
particularly around PPE, just to make a comment.
But Ian, over to you, now.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:37.160
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Just to add a comment on primary
care, picking up on Simon’s point, so, primary

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:45.290
care is now as busy as it was pre-Covid. We
are back to basically over 6 million appointments

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:52.440
a week. And this of course is in a context
of the Covid standard operating procedure

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:58.500
and all of the IPC controls, which have some
kind of productivity hit on primary care.

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:05.820
So, I just really wanted to pay tribute and
recognise the busyness of primary care now,

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:12.570
particularly preceding the winter flu season.
All the more reason why primary care networks

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:19.320
and GP practices are busy hiring additional
staff, and why we collectively, with our partners,

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.100
need to redouble our efforts to hire extra
GPs.

00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:26.480
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Ian. Emily, have
you got any comment on procurement?

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:35.120
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Yes, thank you, David. So,
as you know, DHSC has been focused on five

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:41.290
different areas of supply through the pandemic.
The one that you specifically asked about,

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:48.170
on PPE, the PPE strategy for this part of
the pandemic, was published two days ago and

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:53.430
outlines the substantive stocks that have
been built up of all of the core items of

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:58.830
PPE, as well as the additional resilience
the UK now has because of the additional manufacturing

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:03.660
that is now in place.
So, pre-pandemic, UK only had about 1% manufacture

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:10.600
of the items used in terms of PPE, and now
the capacity is up to 75% overall, which is

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:15.160
a huge amount for some items, and then less
for some others where manufacture will continue

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:20.290
to come from abroad. And most of that stockpile
is already in the UK, so it’s not a concern

00:16:20.290 --> 00:16:24.900
in terms of transport et cetera that there
have been some questions asked about. But

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:28.400
I prefer people to read that document which
has the detail.

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:32.970
And of course, two days ago, we had the report
published by the National Audit Office on

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:38.970
the supply of ventilators similarly, which
shows there are substantive reserves of ventilators

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:44.720
should they be required in the more challenging
scenarios for this winter. And the other areas

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:48.820
around medicines for example, and nonclinical
goods and services, there have been similar

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:53.320
lessons learned from the earlier part of the
pandemic that have been implemented in building

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:58.480
up stockpiles and also just broad supply chain
resilience in terms of relationships with

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:01.990
manufacturing, et cetera.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Emily, thanks very much for

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:05.329
that. That’s great. So, shall we move on
to – Simon, unless you’ve got anything

00:17:05.329 --> 00:17:10.059
else you want to just add to the end of that,
shall we move on to the sort of winter and

00:17:10.059 --> 00:17:16.480
the operational side? I’m going to ask Amanda,
who I can’t see at the moment, but is here.

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:24.579
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you. So, look, that
conversation is a great introduction, actually,

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:31.649
to the paper that we’ve put forward here,
which is covering both arrangements in response

00:17:31.649 --> 00:17:38.690
to a potential second wave of Covid, a comment
in a bit of detail about particularly the

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:43.740
planning over winter, and, of course, the
work to restore non-Covid services that has

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:49.659
been going on. So, thank you for that introduction.
I think just stepping back, Simon mentioned

00:17:49.659 --> 00:17:55.110
that back in July, when he and I wrote out
to the NHS, we talked about this window of

00:17:55.110 --> 00:18:01.450
opportunity, and colleagues have also quite
rightly paid tribute to the extraordinary

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:06.289
efforts of colleagues across the whole of
the NHS, not just in that first wave, but

00:18:06.289 --> 00:18:11.049
really over the last few months as well, because
actually, what we have seen is, people have

00:18:11.049 --> 00:18:17.950
really stepped up to take advantage of that
window of opportunity to restore and recover

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:23.530
the non-Covid work.
And what that means is, as Ian has said, we

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:29.179
are back to pre-pandemic levels of activity
in primary care. As Steve says, there are

00:18:29.179 --> 00:18:33.389
certain things that the NHS, of course, has
never stopped. So, cancer care particularly.

00:18:33.389 --> 00:18:39.040
But, on those areas that we know we had to
make some difficult decisions back in March

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:44.869
to pull back on some of the pure, elective
work, we are now on track to deliver the ambitions

00:18:44.869 --> 00:18:50.510
that we’d set out to get to, you know, somewhere
close to sort of 70% in August, 80% in September.

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:55.500
So, I think it’s a huge reflection on, again,
comments that my colleagues have made about

00:18:55.500 --> 00:19:02.940
actually, how much people have really worked
to get round what is actually, I think we

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:08.659
all have to recognise, really quite difficult
set of circumstances, both in terms of confidence

00:19:08.659 --> 00:19:14.179
in the public to continue to use health services
when they need it, making sure that we are

00:19:14.179 --> 00:19:18.960
absolutely relentlessly applying the infection
control measures to keep patients safe, as

00:19:18.960 --> 00:19:23.289
well as keep our own staff safe, and also
making sure that everything we then do, whether

00:19:23.289 --> 00:19:29.370
it is Covid care, non-Covid care, we do safely,
and that, I think, has been a real success

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:34.529
story over the last few months. So, just to
add my voice to others on thanking colleagues

00:19:34.529 --> 00:19:38.269
for all they have done.
As we move forward, I think let’s be clear,

00:19:38.269 --> 00:19:42.870
though, winter always does bring challenges,
and there is no doubt that if we now think

00:19:42.870 --> 00:19:48.200
about the rising numbers of cases of Covid
that we are seeing, that presents additional

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:53.830
challenges over and above what we would normally
face at this time of year. I’m absolutely

00:19:53.830 --> 00:20:01.010
clear that we are determined to maintain as
much of the non-Covid activity as we safely

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:07.530
can do as we head into winter. Determined
to continue to be open for business, as Steve

00:20:07.530 --> 00:20:16.210
has already said, but absolutely determined
to do all of that safely, and absolutely determined

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:21.670
to support the magnificent effort over the
last few months to do this, to support each

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:26.659
other, to support our colleagues, to do what
we can nationally to support colleagues out

00:20:26.659 --> 00:20:33.110
on the front line and supplement exactly the
sorts of efforts that we are seeing across

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:37.869
the whole NHS around making sure that we have
the right staff, the right skills, the right

00:20:37.869 --> 00:20:44.299
support in place as we head into winter.
So, I won’t say any more. I hope the paper

00:20:44.299 --> 00:20:48.129
sort of speaks for itself in terms of the
detail, but I think perhaps if I just hand

00:20:48.129 --> 00:20:54.220
over to Steve, who could just talking a bit
more detail about where we are and plans going

00:20:54.220 --> 00:20:58.890
forward, and then to Pauline, particularly
to pull out some of the particular detail

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:05.789
around winter planning, if I may, chair.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Amanda.

00:21:05.789 --> 00:21:08.769
Steve.
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: So, I don’t think I’ve

00:21:08.769 --> 00:21:14.129
got a tremendous amount to add to what we’ve
said already in Simon’s introduction and

00:21:14.129 --> 00:21:21.379
in Amanda’s introduction to the paper, and
I’ll pass over to Pauline in a minute. A

00:21:21.379 --> 00:21:28.639
few things that I might highlight. So, I think
on vaccination, so, it’s clearly going into

00:21:28.639 --> 00:21:36.929
winter, where we see flu and other respiratory
illnesses, and so, this year, the flu vaccination

00:21:36.929 --> 00:21:42.710
programme is a really critical part of what
we are doing, and we, and colleagues across

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:48.429
the NHS, community pharmacists, et cetera,
are putting a huge amount of effort into delivering

00:21:48.429 --> 00:21:53.460
those vaccine – that flu vaccine programme.
It is important to remember that that is always

00:21:53.460 --> 00:22:01.390
a programme that lasts from September up until
Christmas, and that we roll out vaccines,

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:07.769
and so vaccine deliveries come during that
period, and GPs and others will be scheduling

00:22:07.769 --> 00:22:15.470
clinics. There is adequate vaccine in the
supply chain to make sure that happens. We

00:22:15.470 --> 00:22:22.139
have not heard of any manufacturing delays,
et cetera. But, it’s really important that

00:22:22.139 --> 00:22:26.940
over the next few months, people do come forward,
and I know the public is already responding

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:32.710
tremendously to this, to get their flu vaccine.
The flu vaccine is effective, it’s a really

00:22:32.710 --> 00:22:39.149
important way of reducing flu during the winter,
and of course, there is clinical evidence

00:22:39.149 --> 00:22:47.179
that flu and Covid together are particularly
– can have a poor outcome. So, we urge people

00:22:47.179 --> 00:22:53.139
to get that flu vaccine, but know that it
is a process that will occur over the next

00:22:53.139 --> 00:22:56.549
few months.
And of course, we are all hopeful that a Covid

00:22:56.549 --> 00:23:03.049
vaccine will come forward, and there are many
vaccines in development, some of which are

00:23:03.049 --> 00:23:08.080
further ahead than others, and we need to
be ready to assist in the deployment of that

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:14.679
once the clinical trials have finished and
the appropriate regulatory approval has been

00:23:14.679 --> 00:23:19.570
granted.
But vaccination, I really want to highlight,

00:23:19.570 --> 00:23:23.869
is a real, particular focus of all of us this
winter.

00:23:23.869 --> 00:23:28.970
And also, just on that, I would say for our
staff, it is particularly important that our

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:34.059
staff are vaccinated against flu this winter.
I have always felt that that is a professional

00:23:34.059 --> 00:23:40.120
obligation. We are obligated, it is quite
clear in the GMC obligations and professional

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:44.989
guidance for doctors that protecting patients
and protecting yourself is important, and

00:23:44.989 --> 00:23:50.419
flu vaccination is a key part of that. So
that is really very important, that our organisations

00:23:50.419 --> 00:23:55.779
work together to ensure that staff are vaccinated
against flu this winter, too.

00:23:55.779 --> 00:23:59.850
Pauline, shall I hand over to you?
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Just before you do, Steve,

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:04.710
just before you hand over to Pauline, could
you just give us a view of the difference

00:24:04.710 --> 00:24:10.850
between now and, say, over the last six months,
with the treatment that patients with Covid

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:14.289
would receive coming into hospital in terms
of, you know, what we have learned over the

00:24:14.289 --> 00:24:17.690
last six months?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Yes. So, we have learnt some

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:23.220
very definite things through clinical trials,
and Simon has referred to this already. So,

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:30.220
the most obvious one is the use of steroids
and dexamethasone was the first of those that

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:36.529
was identified through the recovery trial
that is almost unique to the UK, and I think

00:24:36.529 --> 00:24:41.970
one of the great advantages of the NHS and
this sort of health system is that we can

00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:51.080
rapidly and flexibly put in clinical trials
that produce results which are validated through

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:54.850
the trials process. And that’s exactly what’s
happened. And of course, that’s not just

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:59.509
benefiting NHS patients; it’s benefiting
patients throughout the world. And especially

00:24:59.509 --> 00:25:06.580
because steroid therapy is a cheap, well-established
therapy, so it will benefit people in poorer

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:12.100
countries as well. So, that is a great achievement
that the NHS has been able to facilitate with

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:16.169
our academic partners and National Institute
of Health research.

00:25:16.169 --> 00:25:23.190
And of course, the RECOVERY trial is still
ongoing, and I would urge my colleagues across

00:25:23.190 --> 00:25:29.580
the NHS to encourage people to participate
in those studies, because there is still potential

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:33.609
to get further research information out of
that study.

00:25:33.609 --> 00:25:39.669
It is also true that outside of those trials,
clinicians get used to dealing with diseases

00:25:39.669 --> 00:25:44.480
and new conditions and new viruses and beam
infections. And we can certainly see that

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:50.159
over the last six months, we have learned
an awful lot about how you manage patients,

00:25:50.159 --> 00:25:55.429
about when you ventilate patients, if you
need to, about the sorts of ventilation you

00:25:55.429 --> 00:26:01.481
use, so, so-called non-invasive ventilation
which doesn’t require an anaesthetic. And

00:26:01.481 --> 00:26:07.109
I think you can see that reflected in how
practice has changed and continues to change.

00:26:07.109 --> 00:26:11.020
And of course, there is an international dimension
to this. So, we don’t – you know, we don’t

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:16.200
act alone in this. Across the world, there
are studies, there are reports that our clinicians,

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:21.340
as they always do, referring to, are looking
to, are participating in the work behind.

00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:29.289
And I’m confident as we go forward, we will
learn more about this – this infection.

00:26:29.289 --> 00:26:36.779
So, for instance in areas such as the longer-term
consequences, long Covid, we are learning

00:26:36.779 --> 00:26:44.299
about that at the moment, and our job in the
NHS is to respond rapidly to the needs of

00:26:44.299 --> 00:26:48.240
patients, as the evidence accumulates and
we learn more about Covid.

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:53.950
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Steve. Pauline?
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Thank you very much. So,

00:26:53.950 --> 00:27:00.049
just following on from what Amanda and Steve
have said, and in the paper, I’m just going

00:27:00.049 --> 00:27:05.799
to focus for a minute on section two, which
is around winter operations. And as you can

00:27:05.799 --> 00:27:11.840
see there, we’ve set out a number of work
streams that are important for the coming

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:17.220
weeks and months. They include the work that
Steve has already referred to with regards

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:25.070
to staff vaccination, as we refer to the re-establishment
of our winter operating model to support emergency

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:30.279
care, that is something that we have now been
doing for a number of years, and will be all

00:27:30.279 --> 00:27:38.210
the more important during this period. We
also refer to the work that we are doing as

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:45.090
far as transformation is concerned, both for
emergency care and elective care, and again,

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:52.890
that being increasingly important as the days
and weeks go by, but also ensuring that we

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:59.950
take on board the learning from phase one,
or the first wave of Covid, and that we implement

00:27:59.950 --> 00:28:05.859
those models of care that worked very well,
and how we might do things very differently

00:28:05.859 --> 00:28:10.059
moving forward.
The other thing that we mentioned in that

00:28:10.059 --> 00:28:16.600
section is the issue around capital, and the
capital investments that have been made available

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:23.109
for our emergency departments, and ensuring
that those monies are spent on time so as

00:28:23.109 --> 00:28:27.769
we get maximum benefit.
But, I’d just like to say a few more words

00:28:27.769 --> 00:28:34.379
about testing, which is the second item set
out there. Maybe first to start by saying

00:28:34.379 --> 00:28:40.489
a particular thanks to all of our staff who
work in testing and pathology, et cetera,

00:28:40.489 --> 00:28:44.249
because sometimes these people are the forgotten
heroes, and I’m sure Dido will support me

00:28:44.249 --> 00:28:52.210
with that sentiment. But, we’ve been working
very closely with Test and Trace and with

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:59.039
DHSC, and the important thing for us within
the NHS is to ensure that we continue to secure

00:28:59.039 --> 00:29:05.799
the supplies, platforms, and consumables that
we need to increase the amount of testing

00:29:05.799 --> 00:29:14.120
that we can do for our patients and for our
staff. And if the supplies that we are – well,

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:20.210
that Test and Trace and DHSC are confident
can be secured, do become available during

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:26.629
August – during October, we will see a step
change at the end of that month. So, I think

00:29:26.629 --> 00:29:31.869
that that’s very important to us, and I
know it will be important to all of our patients,

00:29:31.869 --> 00:29:37.320
whether they are emergency patients, elective
patients, and for all of our staff.

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:44.269
I think much has also been said about the
testing of asymptomatic staff, and just to

00:29:44.269 --> 00:29:50.440
confirm that at the moment, within the NHS,
we do test some asymptomatic staff and staff

00:29:50.440 --> 00:29:57.490
working in areas of high prevalence. We will
be very supportive, as government policy emerges,

00:29:57.490 --> 00:30:07.879
on asymptomatic staff testing, and as Test
and Trace developed their plans around being

00:30:07.879 --> 00:30:08.879
able to secure sufficient capacity to support
that.

00:30:08.879 --> 00:30:17.889
I think that’s all I need to say for today.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Dido, you wanted to come in.

00:30:17.889 --> 00:30:23.279
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Yes. Thank you, David. I just
wanted to add to Pauline’s comments and

00:30:23.279 --> 00:30:31.730
to add my thanks to the staff working across
the country in NHS laboratories, PHE laboratories,

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:36.019
and a variety of our other laboratories across
the country. As Pauline says, often quite

00:30:36.019 --> 00:30:43.580
unsung heroes who are working quite literally
24/7 to keep expanding the testing capacity

00:30:43.580 --> 00:30:49.720
across the nation and to keep us all safe.
So, a real heartfelt thanks. And also, to

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:55.710
reiterate, as Pauline said, this is very much
a team effort as we expand the four nations’

00:30:55.710 --> 00:31:01.090
testing capacity to 500,000 tests a day by
the end of October, and to sort of thank Pauline

00:31:01.090 --> 00:31:06.349
and her team for the collaborative way that
we are working together to deliver that. And

00:31:06.349 --> 00:31:08.989
it’s very encouraging to see the progress
being made.

00:31:08.989 --> 00:31:13.940
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Dido. Wol.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thank you, David. Pauline, I

00:31:13.940 --> 00:31:18.179
wonder if you could say a bit more about the
inevitable issues around infection protection

00:31:18.179 --> 00:31:22.909
control, prevention and control? You’re
on mute.

00:31:22.909 --> 00:31:31.350
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Pauline, I think you’re on
mute.

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:39.760
&gt;&gt;Pauline Philip: Yes. Yes. I think you can
hear me now? Sorry. Very happy to comment.

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:46.110
I think Ruth May will probably want to comment
as well. So, from an infection control perspective,

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:52.919
I think we’ve worked to ensure that the
service received timely information as the

00:31:52.919 --> 00:31:59.669
pandemic has progressed, and that is clearly
very important from an emergency care point

00:31:59.669 --> 00:32:05.070
of view, but also from an elective point of
view, so is that staff are very much aware

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:12.269
of the precautions that need to be taken to
ensure that we keep levels of nosocomial infection

00:32:12.269 --> 00:32:18.119
down to an absolute minimum. Indeed, that
we don’t have nosocomial infection, and

00:32:18.119 --> 00:32:24.519
it’s not just from a policy perspective,
but we’ve also clearly made a significant

00:32:24.519 --> 00:32:29.640
investment, I mentioned the work around our
emergency departments, to try and ensure that

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:36.440
we have the appropriate environments that
support staff in their efforts. Ruth may want

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:40.590
to continue?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you very much, chair. So,

00:32:40.590 --> 00:32:46.379
while we know that infection prevention and
control is everyone’s business within the

00:32:46.379 --> 00:32:53.479
workplace, it is of clinicians, managers,
all of our auxiliary staff, everyone’s responsibility.

00:32:53.479 --> 00:32:59.740
And that’s why we’ve worked with PHE as
they’ve published their guidance on infection

00:32:59.740 --> 00:33:05.690
prevention and control, and we’ve updated
it as the pandemic has gone on. So, as we’ve

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:10.639
learned more about the interventions that
we can make, we’ve done that. And masks

00:33:10.639 --> 00:33:15.929
is a really good example.
So, when we saw staff to staff transmission,

00:33:15.929 --> 00:33:22.159
not in the actual clinical workplace, they
were using PPE very, very effectively, keeping

00:33:22.159 --> 00:33:29.049
themselves and their patients safe, what we
did see is in the emotional labour, the emotional

00:33:29.049 --> 00:33:35.870
challenges of being a frontline clinician,
in the coffee rooms, in the offices, people

00:33:35.870 --> 00:33:41.059
weren’t necessarily social distancing. And
that’s why masks were part of our support

00:33:41.059 --> 00:33:46.889
package to staff and their guidance. I’m
really proud of the way that staff have supported

00:33:46.889 --> 00:33:55.149
IPC. They’ve had to really step up and work
with these new – this new virus. Social

00:33:55.149 --> 00:33:59.519
distancing in hospital was nothing that we’ve
seen before. I’m really proud, and I’m

00:33:59.519 --> 00:34:05.039
very, very thankful to the way that all of
our staff have stepped up to do that, particularly

00:34:05.039 --> 00:34:15.179
our IPC – our IPC nurses, doctors, and the
like. I’d like to say also that I’ve been

00:34:15.179 --> 00:34:21.580
really pleased about how we’ve worked in
partnership with our Royal Colleges and our

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:26.080
Royal College of Medical Colleges and our
nursing colleges, and how they’ve helped

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:32.230
us inform the next stages of our guidance.
So, I hope that gives you some information

00:34:32.230 --> 00:34:34.290
of what we are doing.
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: That’s really great. That’s

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:35.290
really encouraging. That’s really encouraging.
Thank you.

00:34:35.290 --> 00:34:41.250
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I wonder, Amanda, will you
just give us the board of view of how hospitals

00:34:41.250 --> 00:34:46.590
– hospital groups and individual hospitals
– are dealing on the ground with this infection

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:51.600
control issue from Covid?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Yes, thank you, David.

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:58.210
So, I think what Ruth and Pauline have said
is absolutely spot-on, and I think there’s

00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:04.250
no doubt that as, and I think Steve has mentioned
it as well, as time has gone on, of course,

00:35:04.250 --> 00:35:10.820
we’ve learned so much more about this virus
and about the steps that we need to take,

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:16.680
colleagues need to take, to keep staff save,
to keep patients safe, so there’s been a

00:35:16.680 --> 00:35:21.640
kind of constant agility that we’ve been
asking for in our kind of frontline colleagues

00:35:21.640 --> 00:35:28.160
to implement the adapted, new, up-to-date
guidance. And real credit, I think, to the

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:33.060
leaders, clinical leaders and managerial colleagues
in how they’ve been able to do that. As

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:36.890
well, as Ruth says, the staff themselves and
how they’ve responded to it.

00:35:36.890 --> 00:35:42.250
I think what we’ve seen, again, what’s
been great is, this didn’t stop at the end

00:35:42.250 --> 00:35:46.860
of the first wave. What we saw in that first
wave, though, was a real coming together of

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:53.740
organisations, hospitals in partnership with
community, mental health, primary care, other

00:35:53.740 --> 00:36:03.060
hospitals, to work as – in a very collaborative
way, whether that was around just very practical

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:07.320
mutual aid, or whether it was that which I
think is the bit where you were driving at,

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:12.600
actually around sharing expertise. So, we’ve
got some great examples from our regions of

00:36:12.600 --> 00:36:17.590
where people have been using peer review,
for example. So, rather than just relying

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:21.910
on self-assessment, around the ability to
comply with infection control standards, they

00:36:21.910 --> 00:36:25.770
have been inviting in colleagues from other
organisations to come and give a fresh eyes

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:30.510
approach and offer some expertise, as well
as course as using regional teams and national

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:33.580
teams to assist with that.
So, we’ve seen that throughout the whole

00:36:33.580 --> 00:36:38.360
of the first wave of the pandemic. I think
we’ve seen it increasingly now bedded in

00:36:38.360 --> 00:36:41.880
as a way of working over the summer.
So, as people have responded to the challenge

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:46.870
to restore services, it’s been done on a
systemwide basis, rather than as individual

00:36:46.870 --> 00:36:51.920
organisations working together, and I think
that is as true of how people have responded

00:36:51.920 --> 00:36:56.620
to infection-control challenges as it is of
how people have responded to elective restarts,

00:36:56.620 --> 00:37:02.710
and it’s very much, I think, what we are
looking to continue to see as, I think Simon

00:37:02.710 --> 00:37:08.530
said it right at the beginning in the meeting,
we see an increasing Covid now, which of course

00:37:08.530 --> 00:37:13.161
is disproportionately affecting some areas
rather than others, so the need to continue

00:37:13.161 --> 00:37:18.540
to have that systemwide response, you know,
is critical to our ability to continue to

00:37:18.540 --> 00:37:20.620
provide services safely over the next six
months.

00:37:20.620 --> 00:37:25.340
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Amanda. Look, before
I hand over to Julian on the money, is there

00:37:25.340 --> 00:37:29.340
anything else you wanted to say, before I
do that, from an operational perspective?

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:33.890
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: For me, no, I think it
was really – so, thank you for the opportunity.

00:37:33.890 --> 00:37:40.800
I think the message really is, as ever, the
headline is, actually, it’s just a massive

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:46.060
thank you, because whether that is to people
working on the front lines, the leaders within

00:37:46.060 --> 00:37:49.950
individual organisations, to those working
at system level, regional level, national

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:56.140
level, it still feels like this is a huge
combined effort to make sure that the NHS

00:37:56.140 --> 00:38:02.080
is open for business as it has been throughout,
that we are continuing to maintain, protect

00:38:02.080 --> 00:38:07.250
the non-Covid work, but crucially now of course
turning our attention to safely being able

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:11.480
to respond to increases in pressure, whether
that sort of normal winter pressures or it’s

00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:16.470
the additional pressures that Covid brings.
And that, I think, really probably in a nutshell,

00:38:16.470 --> 00:38:21.580
David, is where I think the NHS has been,
is now, and is looking to the future.

00:38:21.580 --> 00:38:25.700
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Okay. Thank you very
much, Amanda, for that. Thank you. Julian,

00:38:25.700 --> 00:38:33.940
where are we with the numbers?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: So, as the report sets out,

00:38:33.940 --> 00:38:40.890
which takes us through to the end of July,
there’s been a very consistent rate of aggregate

00:38:40.890 --> 00:38:46.600
[inaudible] over the first four months of
the year. Subsequent to this report, we have

00:38:46.600 --> 00:38:54.240
had month five numbers, which actually just
continue that trend. On average, we have effectively

00:38:54.240 --> 00:39:03.310
been spending about £1.25 billion more a
month than the budget planned before the start

00:39:03.310 --> 00:39:09.730
of the year. That is a combination of higher
costs due to Covid as we expand the workforce,

00:39:09.730 --> 00:39:17.090
we dealt with sick absence, lower income from
providers, as they’ve had less private patient

00:39:17.090 --> 00:39:22.810
income, less visitors into hospital, changes
in their research programmes, there’s been

00:39:22.810 --> 00:39:29.870
a whole series of issues. The cost of the
extra services, which we – the extra capacity

00:39:29.870 --> 00:39:35.790
we have put in place through the independent
sector, and the enhanced discharge arrangements

00:39:35.790 --> 00:39:40.340
that have been put in place. And certainly,
at the beginning of this period, actually,

00:39:40.340 --> 00:39:45.490
higher levels of prescribing, as we went into
the kind of first phase of lockdown. But the

00:39:45.490 --> 00:39:51.810
pattern has remained remarkably constant,
even as we’ve now stepped activity backup.

00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:57.960
I mean, you can see a change in costs due
to Covid versus the extra marginal costs of

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:04.990
activity.
Worth saying that we have put out to the NHS

00:40:04.990 --> 00:40:11.040
financial envelopes for the second half of
the year. Consistent with the planning for

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:17.290
recovery that Amanda and Pauline have mentioned,
we are now getting back the first financial

00:40:17.290 --> 00:40:26.040
returns consistent with those financial envelopes,
and we will continue to track how that works

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:31.370
through – over the next month. I should
probably stop there and just take any questions.

00:40:31.370 --> 00:40:38.060
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Julian.
Anyone on the board have any questions for

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:43.880
Julian on the numbers? Wol, as chairman of
the [inaudible], do you have any questions?

00:40:43.880 --> 00:40:50.220
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Actually, I don’t. It’s
a very good paper, Julian, thanks. It’s

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:55.410
very clear what’s happening. Perhaps just
one. How comfortable and confident are you

00:40:55.410 --> 00:41:04.430
about the balance of incentives to continuing
work in the electives and non-Covid area versus

00:41:04.430 --> 00:41:13.231
the fact that we are having to sort of address
Covid? How comfortable are you that the balance

00:41:13.231 --> 00:41:14.231
is right?
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: I think is kind of Pauline

00:41:14.231 --> 00:41:18.770
and Amanda have said, you really do – you
know, we have really seen over the last couple

00:41:18.770 --> 00:41:24.980
of months the service work incredibly hard
to step activity backup while still dealing

00:41:24.980 --> 00:41:30.630
with some of the issues around infection control.
You still see it now even as some of the sick

00:41:30.630 --> 00:41:38.270
absence has ticked backup. So, it’s a – you
know, massive challenge, but you can really

00:41:38.270 --> 00:41:43.110
see the service responding to recover.
You know, clearly, there’s going to be a

00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:48.040
challenge as we go through this next phase,
depending on what happens to the pattern of

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:53.670
Covid, but I think, again, you can see the
plans coming through to where the service

00:41:53.670 --> 00:41:57.720
is really thinking hard about how it uses
the capacity it has available, the independent

00:41:57.720 --> 00:42:05.270
sector, where people are thinking of switching,
you know, particular pathways, for example

00:42:05.270 --> 00:42:11.400
into the independent sector to protect them
as we, you know, depending on what happens

00:42:11.400 --> 00:42:18.900
to Covid in local systems. We have put out
there a financial incentive scheme, not without

00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:27.160
some controversy, but again, I think you can
see the service responding and doing, you

00:42:27.160 --> 00:42:30.680
know, absolutely working flat out to recover
those services.

00:42:30.680 --> 00:42:36.720
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Julian. Any other
questions on the first four months’ results?

00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:44.070
No? Okay. Well, thank you. So, the next item
is an item very dear to my heart, and so,

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:49.330
Ian, you’ve done a fabulous paper on this
on getting to net zero carbon by 2040. Would

00:42:49.330 --> 00:42:53.780
you like to introduce the paper and introduce
Nick Watts as well? I think Nick, you’ve

00:42:53.780 --> 00:42:58.890
just joined us, I think.
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: I have. I’m here.

00:42:58.890 --> 00:43:02.238
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ian, over to you.
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Great. Thank you, David. So,

00:43:02.238 --> 00:43:08.731
we protected this work fully during Covid,
and as a result of that, we can today set

00:43:08.731 --> 00:43:16.070
out ambitious goals. These have been warmly
welcomed by the World Health Organisation.

00:43:16.070 --> 00:43:23.610
We intend to use our leadership not just to
look at what’s directly within the NHS as

00:43:23.610 --> 00:43:28.810
gift, the NHS carbon footprint, but also how
we can influence our supply chains, including

00:43:28.810 --> 00:43:37.060
for example medicines. The goals in the report
are as informed as they can be by the evidence.

00:43:37.060 --> 00:43:43.550
We had an international call with extensive
engagement. Consideration of different interventions,

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:48.680
and a hell of a lot of detailed modelling
of all of the different effects. The report

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:53.570
marks a major step, and of course, it’s
not the last word. It couldn’t be in this

00:43:53.570 --> 00:43:58.330
arena. We are inviting feedback and welcome
further suggestions. And wherever it is credible

00:43:58.330 --> 00:44:05.790
to do so, we will go further and faster.
I’d like to highlight I think the last mile

00:44:05.790 --> 00:44:12.420
problem. The challenge of getting from 80%
reduction to 100% reduction. This is very

00:44:12.420 --> 00:44:17.740
significant in this area, and that is why
we have developed ambitious earlier goals

00:44:17.740 --> 00:44:27.090
of that 8% reduction, as well as the net zero
goal. Prior to COP 26, the UN conference that

00:44:27.090 --> 00:44:31.550
the government will be hosting next year,
we’ll be working incredibly closely with

00:44:31.550 --> 00:44:37.290
government so that the NHS can take a central
role within the government’s green agenda.

00:44:37.290 --> 00:44:41.710
And my final observation is that as we are
focusing on delivering, I do think we can

00:44:41.710 --> 00:44:50.190
take some comfort from our track record in
this area since 1990, and the progress we’ve

00:44:50.190 --> 00:44:57.960
made against our climate change act commitments,
and also the huge energy and enthusiasm we

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:06.990
see of frontline NHS staff to participate
and progress this agenda. We are strengthening

00:45:06.990 --> 00:45:14.460
our own capabilities under Nick’s leadership,
so Nick not only has chaired the panel and

00:45:14.460 --> 00:45:18.740
been the executive director of the Lancet
Countdown, he is now joining us full-time

00:45:18.740 --> 00:45:26.010
from the 26th of this month as the NHS’s
chief sustainability officer. And we have

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:31.720
a lot of input and support, fantastic support
from teams across NHS E&amp;I, including perhaps

00:45:31.720 --> 00:45:36.380
most obviously, Emily’s team that is working
on estates and also on supplies.

00:45:36.380 --> 00:45:41.770
So, I will turn now to Nick to take us through
the item, but as I do so, I just want to say

00:45:41.770 --> 00:45:46.630
a big thank you, Nick, to you for your personal
leadership to the Lancet Countdown, for all

00:45:46.630 --> 00:45:53.250
the resources they’ve provided, gratis in
some instances, to help with this work, to

00:45:53.250 --> 00:45:59.890
my team, and indeed panel members. Nick.
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Indeed. Thank you. The thanks

00:45:59.890 --> 00:46:05.690
doesn’t go to me. The thanks goes to the
team at NHS E&amp;I that have really been working

00:46:05.690 --> 00:46:10.690
behind this. It has been incredibly impressive.
The thanks, though, also goes, Ian, to yourself,

00:46:10.690 --> 00:46:15.770
to David, to Simon, for your support.
Everything that you’re going to hear, I’m

00:46:15.770 --> 00:46:20.880
only going to take five minutes. Everything
you will hear has taken place over the last

00:46:20.880 --> 00:46:26.560
nine months while the NHS has been responding
to Covid. So, everything that we are discussing

00:46:26.560 --> 00:46:30.002
and everything in the report that’s being
sent there is designed with this in mind.

00:46:30.002 --> 00:46:34.450
It is conscious of the pressures, conscious
of the response of the system, and conscious

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:40.300
of ensuring that as we work towards delivering
a net zero health service, to respond to climate

00:46:40.300 --> 00:46:45.800
change, it is also done in a way that is good
for patients, good for public health.

00:46:45.800 --> 00:46:52.050
So, I’m a doctor by training. I work on
the link between public health and climate

00:46:52.050 --> 00:46:57.750
change. I worked with the WHO, with the Lancet,
with University College London. I’ve spent

00:46:57.750 --> 00:47:04.440
my life working with an enormous amount of
data. In three very clear things are apparent

00:47:04.440 --> 00:47:07.950
when you look at the data.
The first thing. The human symptoms of climate

00:47:07.950 --> 00:47:12.510
change, they are unequivocal. They are affecting
the health of the British public today and

00:47:12.510 --> 00:47:17.460
right around the world.
Secondly, at the global level, not enough

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:22.310
is being done. Not enough is being done to
respond to this emerging health crisis.

00:47:22.310 --> 00:47:28.740
And thirdly, that response, where it does
exist, is fundamentally good for public health.

00:47:28.740 --> 00:47:32.990
And there are glimmers of help and reasons
for optimism. And I think the thing I’d

00:47:32.990 --> 00:47:38.210
like to start with is that, for the last decade
or so, the NHS in England has always been

00:47:38.210 --> 00:47:42.570
one of those reasons for hope. When we look
around the world at the response to climate

00:47:42.570 --> 00:47:47.380
change, the rest of the world, it looks at
the NHS with great admiration here. New information

00:47:47.380 --> 00:47:52.540
in this report, where we re-baseline the carbon
emissions of the health service, we see for

00:47:52.540 --> 00:47:57.900
the emissions that we directly control, 62%
reduction in emissions. For the entirety,

00:47:57.900 --> 00:48:02.660
for the emissions that we control as well
as those we influence in the supply chain,

00:48:02.660 --> 00:48:08.070
a 26% reduction. These are very impressive.
They are in line, they exceed the commitments

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:12.500
under the climate change act.
So, it is with that that we build on this

00:48:12.500 --> 00:48:19.230
net zero plan. It’s the result of 650 submissions,
over 650 submissions, to a call for evidence.

00:48:19.230 --> 00:48:24.860
The team of analysts we’ve thanked, guidance
from an expert panel, it builds on that, and

00:48:24.860 --> 00:48:28.330
it answers a pretty simple question: what
is the fastest, what is the most credible

00:48:28.330 --> 00:48:34.390
date, that the NHS can reach net zero carbon
emissions? And so, the answer is there for

00:48:34.390 --> 00:48:40.130
the carbon footprint, which is the emissions
we control directly, is 2040. This is the

00:48:40.130 --> 00:48:43.790
earliest, this is the most ambitious. But
still credible.

00:48:43.790 --> 00:48:48.730
To make sure that we have near-term goals
as well, we have an ambition to reach an 80%

00:48:48.730 --> 00:48:54.690
reduction by, the very earliest, 2028. But
as Ian said, we take full accountability for

00:48:54.690 --> 00:48:58.790
the entirety of our footprint and profile,
and so, we’ve developed a second target,

00:48:58.790 --> 00:49:02.810
called in the paper, the carbon footprint
plus. This includes all of the emissions we

00:49:02.810 --> 00:49:07.590
influence, as well as those that happen on
site in the delivery of care. And there, we

00:49:07.590 --> 00:49:12.920
think we can reach net zero by 2045, with
an ambition again to reach an 80% reduction

00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:18.150
by 2036 at the very earliest.
So, the report lays out all the modelling

00:49:18.150 --> 00:49:24.230
for this. It lays out all the interventions
behind it, it lays out the thinking. It describes

00:49:24.230 --> 00:49:28.990
the pace, the scale and the scope of change
we think that is needed. But most importantly

00:49:28.990 --> 00:49:34.390
I think, it describes three key elements that
we need to make sure that we are successful.

00:49:34.390 --> 00:49:39.630
So, firstly, it’s important that we get
started now. It’s important that there are

00:49:39.630 --> 00:49:44.400
early commitments to make sure that we demonstrate
that the NHS is serious and that it’s credible

00:49:44.400 --> 00:49:48.650
across our entire emissions profile, and you
will see reference to new hospital standards,

00:49:48.650 --> 00:49:54.080
with net zero hospitals. You can see the launch
of a new national greener NHS programme, and

00:49:54.080 --> 00:49:58.780
you will see every single part of the emissions
profile of the NHS with work to do.

00:49:58.780 --> 00:50:03.540
It definitely has to do, it has to lay out
some of those interim targets and it have

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:09.480
to launch an iterative and adaptive process.
2045, 25 years away, is a long way away, and

00:50:09.480 --> 00:50:13.000
we don’t have all the answers right now.
We are committing to get to that, and the

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:17.940
point that we are aware that we will have
to come back again and again, and so in line

00:50:17.940 --> 00:50:22.870
with the carbon budgets of the United Kingdom,
will come back and reassess, look at how technology

00:50:22.870 --> 00:50:28.350
has shifted, look at how clinicians, health
staff, how the NHS has responded to this challenge,

00:50:28.350 --> 00:50:31.730
and, where possible, where practicable, increase
our ambition.

00:50:31.730 --> 00:50:38.160
Finally, the third thing that this needs to
be a success, is this is just a report. It

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:42.470
needs the support of all 1.3 million of the
NHS staff, and it needs the support of each

00:50:42.470 --> 00:50:48.290
of you in order to be successful. We know
from a recent survey that 98% of NHS staff

00:50:48.290 --> 00:50:52.970
support the health service taking further
action on this agenda. We also know that,

00:50:52.970 --> 00:50:58.440
over the last 10 years, and from a wealth
of scientific literature across the world,

00:50:58.440 --> 00:51:01.810
actions to respond to climate change they
are fundamentally good for health. They result

00:51:01.810 --> 00:51:06.730
in cleaner air, they result in healthier diets,
they result in physical activity and safer

00:51:06.730 --> 00:51:11.250
communities. Much of what we are talking about
here will improve patient care, it will increase

00:51:11.250 --> 00:51:16.940
efficiencies, and it will prepare us for the
future. And so, with those three things in

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:21.220
mind, and the launch of an engagement process
over the next 6 to 12 months, I look forward

00:51:21.220 --> 00:51:24.960
to the discussion over the next couple of
minutes of the time we have left. The targets

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:30.250
and the commitments here, they are credible,
they are ambitious, they will ensure the NHS

00:51:30.250 --> 00:51:34.880
is able to continue to deliver high quality
care for all, both today but well into the

00:51:34.880 --> 00:51:38.900
future. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Nick, thank you very much for

00:51:38.900 --> 00:51:43.190
that. I mean, we are – it’s – it’s
a very exciting programme. David Roberts,

00:51:43.190 --> 00:51:48.140
you – you had an interesting perspective
on this from a staff point of view.

00:51:48.140 --> 00:51:53.610
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: So, I’ve got a perspective,
and then if I may, a question. So, my perspective

00:51:53.610 --> 00:51:59.660
is, you know, I think this is – so, the
work – the places I work outside of the

00:51:59.660 --> 00:52:08.830
NHS, this is vital, because actually, if you
don’t respond to the climate emergency,

00:52:08.830 --> 00:52:15.140
then your people will slowly but surely lose
confidence in you. And actually, from a recruitment

00:52:15.140 --> 00:52:19.360
perspective, from a retention perspective,
from the motivation and morale perspective,

00:52:19.360 --> 00:52:25.230
all of those things mean that we don’t have
an option to engage our people and support

00:52:25.230 --> 00:52:30.220
them in following the actions and following
through. So, I think this is an incredibly

00:52:30.220 --> 00:52:36.480
powerful platform across multiple stakeholders.
My question, Nick, is the other thing – the

00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:40.750
other places I do. Scope one and two, you
know, broadly within your control, you can

00:52:40.750 --> 00:52:46.640
access it. Scope three, really, really hard.
And therefore, the question for you is, how

00:52:46.640 --> 00:52:51.470
credible is there only to be five-year gap
between scope two and scope three, given the

00:52:51.470 --> 00:52:56.140
challenges for scope two?
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Yeah, that’s right. You’re

00:52:56.140 --> 00:53:01.820
picking up on a crucial issue there about
direct control, and sort of the issues we

00:53:01.820 --> 00:53:09.150
can – we can influence. Part of what makes
that 2045 ambition there credible is that

00:53:09.150 --> 00:53:14.540
the rest of the world is moving with us. We
are seeing all around the world countries,

00:53:14.540 --> 00:53:19.620
companies, communities, bringing forward their
own net zero commitments. And as we do that,

00:53:19.620 --> 00:53:23.990
supply chains will decarbonise along with
us. And so, this is in line with the direction

00:53:23.990 --> 00:53:28.650
of travel for the international community,
and that’s what helps it. We are moving

00:53:28.650 --> 00:53:34.280
ahead of that. We are pushing the curve there,
but it is that joint commitment that helps

00:53:34.280 --> 00:53:38.170
us get there. This is the NHS’ sort of role
in that leadership.

00:53:38.170 --> 00:53:42.460
In terms of the five-year gap between the
two, I suppose the reason that is credible

00:53:42.460 --> 00:53:46.770
is because of the Paris Agreement, because
of how powerful that international agreement

00:53:46.770 --> 00:53:51.120
is. It’s actually what the WHO has called
one of the most powerful public-health treaties

00:53:51.120 --> 00:53:56.821
of the last century. That sets us on that
trajectory as well, and, again, as the world

00:53:56.821 --> 00:54:02.790
comes to COP 26 in Glasgow in 2021, we think
that, in response to this commitment, we will

00:54:02.790 --> 00:54:06.720
start to see other systems, we hope we will
start to see other health systems, move with

00:54:06.720 --> 00:54:08.780
us.
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Okay. And you’ve seen China

00:54:08.780 --> 00:54:10.770
and others giving leadership recently?
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Indeed.

00:54:10.770 --> 00:54:13.210
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Yeah. Okay. Thank you. Thank
you, David.

00:54:13.210 --> 00:54:17.530
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Emily, clearly, your responsibilities
for both property and the supply chain are

00:54:17.530 --> 00:54:23.520
going to be crucial to this. To what extent
have you got – are you really involved with

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:29.720
this project?
&gt;&gt;Emily Lawson: Both Nick’s team, rather,

00:54:29.720 --> 00:54:38.100
and my team, are working very closely together
on this. So, in two key areas, but there are

00:54:38.100 --> 00:54:43.490
several mentioned in the paper. The first
is on the whole supply chain peace. We are

00:54:43.490 --> 00:54:48.390
obviously working closely there with DHSC
and NHS Supply Chain, who hold critical parts

00:54:48.390 --> 00:54:53.530
of the supply chain, to make sure that this
is engineered throughout our commercial activity.

00:54:53.530 --> 00:55:00.390
And the second is in our infrastructure management.
So, part of that is obviously with NHS Property

00:55:00.390 --> 00:55:06.190
Services, and community health partnerships,
but part of it is with trusts and with other

00:55:06.190 --> 00:55:13.500
healthcare users. So, the big opportunities
include how we approach the 40 hospitals programme,

00:55:13.500 --> 00:55:17.760
the infrastructure programme, but also, some
of the other infrastructure investments that

00:55:17.760 --> 00:55:21.970
are being made in the short term, for example,
addressing the mental health [inaudible] position.

00:55:21.970 --> 00:55:27.310
And, so then the numbers which are in the
paper, particularly the helpful chart with

00:55:27.310 --> 00:55:32.040
the curves that shows everything we have to
do to get to net zero includes the expertise

00:55:32.040 --> 00:55:36.860
of those teams, as to what can be achieved
over this period.

00:55:36.860 --> 00:55:43.980
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Emily. Wol?
&gt;&gt;Wol Kolade: Thank you, chair. The hospital

00:55:43.980 --> 00:55:51.340
infrastructure is not homogenous, in the sense
that urban settings versus rural settings,

00:55:51.340 --> 00:55:56.110
or less heavily urbanised settings, and I’ve
heard it described that in very urban settings

00:55:56.110 --> 00:56:01.690
that hospitals are the – you know, the modern
equivalent of factories in the centres of

00:56:01.690 --> 00:56:07.510
areas, generating pollution and traffic. Have
you thought about the pace at which you can

00:56:07.510 --> 00:56:12.740
affect the existing infrastructure as opposed
to the new hospitals which are being built?

00:56:12.740 --> 00:56:19.210
And by the way, fantastic report, it’s unbelievably
great that the NHS are leading on this.

00:56:19.210 --> 00:56:24.500
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Thank you. A good question.
Yes, we absolutely have – have thought through

00:56:24.500 --> 00:56:29.800
this, and if it’s helpful, at some point,
we can provide the detail attached to that.

00:56:29.800 --> 00:56:34.580
It is important that we are able to decarbonise
not only new infrastructure, you’re right,

00:56:34.580 --> 00:56:40.000
but the existing estate. I think the issue
that you picked up on there is probably exactly

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:44.650
why we think this is going to be positive.
Great Ormond Street Hospital in London is

00:56:44.650 --> 00:56:49.690
a fantastic example of shutting down every
now and then its roads, letting kids in the

00:56:49.690 --> 00:56:54.420
hospital, out and play and enjoy the space.
That’s great for them, because it gives

00:56:54.420 --> 00:56:59.510
them a bit of space outside of the hospital.
It’s good for air pollution as well. It

00:56:59.510 --> 00:57:03.740
drastically reduces our pollution, and we
know the 30,000-40,000 deaths every year that

00:57:03.740 --> 00:57:10.180
happen as a result of that. So, it is exactly
those health benefits that come from decarbonising

00:57:10.180 --> 00:57:14.720
the existing estate, particularly in highly
dense urban settings, that I think gives us

00:57:14.720 --> 00:57:18.610
the confidence to be able to do that. There
is a lot of work to do there, though. The

00:57:18.610 --> 00:57:24.750
NHS has a big estate. Thankfully, it has the
workforce, exactly as you said, David, that

00:57:24.750 --> 00:57:29.190
I think will be behind this to help deliver
it.

00:57:29.190 --> 00:57:34.520
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Munir.
&gt;&gt;Munir Pirmohamed: Thank you very much. Fantastic

00:57:34.520 --> 00:57:38.960
report and fully supported. As you said, it
is affecting public health right now and we

00:57:38.960 --> 00:57:45.180
need to do something about it. I just want
to, you know, and you rightly have highlighted

00:57:45.180 --> 00:57:48.510
the importance of research and innovation
that may be really important to get the last

00:57:48.510 --> 00:57:52.780
20%. I wondered whether you can elaborate
on how that will be important.

00:57:52.780 --> 00:57:57.920
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Yeah. This is that last mile
problem in part that Ian helpfully alluded

00:57:57.920 --> 00:58:01.890
to. We must always keep that in mind. The
approach you have to take, I think, the approach

00:58:01.890 --> 00:58:07.250
at the international level for reducing emissions
and hitting net zero targets is, you must

00:58:07.250 --> 00:58:11.661
reduce your emissions as far as you credibly
can. And then you must reduce them just a

00:58:11.661 --> 00:58:15.970
little bit further, and then you can start
to consider what we call negative emissions.

00:58:15.970 --> 00:58:20.850
And this, then, is when you start to look
at ways that you can take the emissions outside

00:58:20.850 --> 00:58:25.560
of the atmosphere.
So, there is a thing called the NHS Forest.

00:58:25.560 --> 00:58:30.980
We know that access to green space improves
mental health. We know that it’s good for

00:58:30.980 --> 00:58:36.860
physical activity. We know that it reduces
noise, air pollution. Those sorts of interventions

00:58:36.860 --> 00:58:40.950
are the kinds of things available to us to
access some of those negative emissions. And

00:58:40.950 --> 00:58:45.810
then, on top of that, there is the research
and the innovation space there. There will

00:58:45.810 --> 00:58:49.830
be technologies that do not exist today, that
we can’t even imagine, but they certainly

00:58:49.830 --> 00:58:55.600
will be there going forward. Now, the reason
we know this, or the reason I have a little

00:58:55.600 --> 00:58:59.780
bit of confidence in saying that is that we
have seen this happen again and again. We

00:58:59.780 --> 00:59:04.510
have seen targets in response to the 2008
climate change act move forward again and

00:59:04.510 --> 00:59:12.430
again as technology evolves. Previously, it
was unfeasible, the idea that electric and

00:59:12.430 --> 00:59:18.140
non-electric vehicles would be at cost parity
until the mid-2030s. That’s already happened.

00:59:18.140 --> 00:59:21.650
That technology advanced rapidly. And so,
that’s the kind of research and innovation

00:59:21.650 --> 00:59:24.450
that we expect to see, and hopefully expect
to lead.

00:59:24.450 --> 00:59:30.840
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you. Ruth.
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you very much, Nick. Great,

00:59:30.840 --> 00:59:37.540
really energising and exciting stuff. So thank
you very much for the work that you are leading.

00:59:37.540 --> 00:59:43.680
I know that my profession, the biggest workforce
in the NHS, would be really behind this, and

00:59:43.680 --> 00:59:48.350
I’m sure we could provide you and give you
some experts to join your export panel as

00:59:48.350 --> 00:59:53.190
well, so that nurses can make a contribution
at all levels through the next stages of that.

00:59:53.190 --> 00:59:56.090
I look forward to working with you, Nick.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Excellent.

00:59:56.090 --> 00:59:59.620
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: David, that wasn’t a question,
but do you mind terribly if I quickly respond?

00:59:59.620 --> 01:00:03.040
&gt;&gt;David Prior: No, of course. Go ahead.
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Ruth, thank you for the offer.

01:00:03.040 --> 01:00:09.790
We would love to help. Nurses across the world
and especially in England have led the charge

01:00:09.790 --> 01:00:13.210
in the response to climate change. The work
of the Royal College of Nursing is incredibly

01:00:13.210 --> 01:00:18.530
impressive. The work of the profession across
the different trusts. So, I think we are fully

01:00:18.530 --> 01:00:21.990
aware of that, and we would welcome any help
you can provide.

01:00:21.990 --> 01:00:25.660
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Look forward to it, Nick.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Simon, you’re going

01:00:25.660 --> 01:00:30.420
to have to have the last word on this, I think,
please.

01:00:30.420 --> 01:00:39.431
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, thank you, Nick, and
thank you to the entire 600 plus people who

01:00:39.431 --> 01:00:43.280
have responded to your call for evidence,
and all of the staff across the health service

01:00:43.280 --> 01:00:47.500
who have led to this. I thought, may be David,
just to summarise three key points that I

01:00:47.500 --> 01:00:52.250
think came out of the conversation.
The first is that, of course, 2020 has been

01:00:52.250 --> 01:00:57.991
dominated by coronavirus. That is the highest
immediate priority and pressure on the health

01:00:57.991 --> 01:01:04.980
service, as we all know. It was just sort
of bad luck for Nick that it was a week after

01:01:04.980 --> 01:01:11.000
his work was commissioned that the WHO declared
coronavirus of global health emergency. But

01:01:11.000 --> 01:01:16.990
the reason we feel that we have to do both
and is that looking out not just now but over

01:01:16.990 --> 01:01:22.150
the medium and long term, it is clear that
the evidence shows climate change will be

01:01:22.150 --> 01:01:26.460
the biggest threat to human health in the
way that Nick has described.

01:01:26.460 --> 01:01:30.360
And the second thing that follows from that
of course is that the NHS is both part of

01:01:30.360 --> 01:01:34.570
the problem as well as potentially part of
the solution. We are the biggest employer

01:01:34.570 --> 01:01:40.570
in the country. We are perhaps 4-5% of [inaudible]
in this country, and therefore if the UK is

01:01:40.570 --> 01:01:47.161
going to meet its legally binding climate
obligations, the NHS is going to have to continue

01:01:47.161 --> 01:01:51.290
to step up.
And I think the point that Nick makes about

01:01:51.290 --> 01:01:56.530
the international aspects of this are particularly
significant. Because, to the question that

01:01:56.530 --> 01:02:02.240
David Roberts raised, actually, we are going
to have to catalyse changes across the healthcare

01:02:02.240 --> 01:02:07.940
supply chain including the international pharmaceutical
sector, and that is one of the reasons why

01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:14.540
it is particularly welcome today that the
World Health Organisation, the director general,

01:02:14.540 --> 01:02:20.840
Dr Tedros Ghebreyesus, has specifically come
out and backed the work that the NHS is leading

01:02:20.840 --> 01:02:24.330
thanks to Nick’s report, because that will
help catalyse some of those international

01:02:24.330 --> 01:02:29.890
partnerships with our shared supply chains.
And then, I think the third thing to say is

01:02:29.890 --> 01:02:37.980
that there will inevitably be a debate about,
on the one hand, the credibility of the stretch

01:02:37.980 --> 01:02:43.849
targets that are being set here, versus, on
the other, whether or not we can go faster.

01:02:43.849 --> 01:02:51.080
And the whole point of having this laid out
in such a detailed way is so that we can actually

01:02:51.080 --> 01:02:57.510
have that debate, we can mobilise support,
and we can continue to deepen ambition.

01:02:57.510 --> 01:03:02.330
So, I think these are a set of firm commitments
we are seeking to enter into, but this will

01:03:02.330 --> 01:03:06.770
be an ongoing process, and by no means is
today the final word.

01:03:06.770 --> 01:03:10.980
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Simon. So, Nick,
you’ve got the total endorsement of the

01:03:10.980 --> 01:03:16.130
board, and huge enthusiasm from us all. And
so, all power to your elbow.

01:03:16.130 --> 01:03:21.940
&gt;&gt;Nick Watts: Thank you. Thank you, everyone.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Nick. So,

01:03:21.940 --> 01:03:27.340
the next item is diagnostics, and I’m very
pleased to welcome my old colleague Mike Richards

01:03:27.340 --> 01:03:34.340
back here, who’s done this outstanding report
on diagnostics. I can’t see you yet, Mike,

01:03:34.340 --> 01:03:37.360
but are you here?
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: I’m here. I am here. I

01:03:37.360 --> 01:03:39.570
promise.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. Because when we last

01:03:39.570 --> 01:03:43.260
spoke, you weren’t feeling terribly well.
So, anyway, you are here and with us, which

01:03:43.260 --> 01:03:45.420
is terrific. So, would you like to present
your report?

01:03:45.420 --> 01:03:49.570
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: I would be very happy to
do that, and thank you for inviting me, and

01:03:49.570 --> 01:03:55.020
thank you, Simon, for commissioning the report.
First thing to say is that I’m not going

01:03:55.020 --> 01:03:59.180
to go through it in detail because you’ve
all had it, but it is deliberately broad in

01:03:59.180 --> 01:04:05.980
its scope, covering imaging, endoscopy, pathology,
and physiological measurement, and some aspects

01:04:05.980 --> 01:04:11.570
of genomics as well.
I think you will all know that diagnostics

01:04:11.570 --> 01:04:19.520
is really key to many areas of healthcare,
and indeed to the commitment in the NHS long-term

01:04:19.520 --> 01:04:25.760
plan. The problem is that even before the
pandemic, the diagnostics were reaching a

01:04:25.760 --> 01:04:34.180
tipping point. Demand was exceeding capacity.
Demand was going up from all directions. From

01:04:34.180 --> 01:04:39.940
A&amp;E, from inpatients, from outpatients, from
GP requests. If you go to an A&amp;E department,

01:04:39.940 --> 01:04:46.450
a type one A&amp;E department, one in 10 patients
will get a CT scan, just for example.

01:04:46.450 --> 01:04:53.970
But in comparison with that demand, neither
our equipment nor our workforce have gone

01:04:53.970 --> 01:05:00.330
up at the same pace, and we are seeing that
impact both in terms of waiting times increased,

01:05:00.330 --> 01:05:09.690
but also the need to outsource imaging, image
reporting, endoscopy, and indeed some aspects

01:05:09.690 --> 01:05:13.810
of pathology as well.
And you will also have seen from the report

01:05:13.810 --> 01:05:18.490
that if you look at our international comparisons,
particularly on CT and MRI where we’ve got

01:05:18.490 --> 01:05:24.050
OECD data, we are right at the bottom of an
international league table.

01:05:24.050 --> 01:05:32.880
So, that was the position before Covid. With
Covid, you will all know that throughput has

01:05:32.880 --> 01:05:40.720
been reduced, and we’ve heard about IPC
issues in this meeting this afternoon. The

01:05:40.720 --> 01:05:46.100
backlogs have built up, particularly with
endoscopy. The pathology staff have been working

01:05:46.100 --> 01:05:52.850
really 24 hours a day trying to do the testing.
And Covid itself has had an impact. For a

01:05:52.850 --> 01:05:58.480
start, we’ve seen the impact on mobile chest
x-ray machines, which are needed on intensive-care

01:05:58.480 --> 01:06:04.660
units, showing up the fact that a lot of our
machines are well past 10 years old. And we’ve

01:06:04.660 --> 01:06:10.990
got the new problem of long Covid, with respiratory
problems and cardiac problems, et cetera.

01:06:10.990 --> 01:06:17.830
So, that all the challenges.
The report, not surprisingly, says we need

01:06:17.830 --> 01:06:23.650
to increase capacity. That is, we need to
have more machines and facilities, we need

01:06:23.650 --> 01:06:28.550
to have more workforce, and alongside that,
we need to have the training to get the workforce.

01:06:28.550 --> 01:06:36.010
So, there are both capital and revenue implications.
But as much as that, we need to do things

01:06:36.010 --> 01:06:42.200
differently. And the key recommendation in
the report is that we should separate what

01:06:42.200 --> 01:06:45.160
I’m calling acute diagnostics from elective
diagnostics.

01:06:45.160 --> 01:06:51.160
So, acute diagnostics being those diagnostics
needed by the A&amp;E department or the inpatient

01:06:51.160 --> 01:06:56.480
wards, where, whatever the test is, it needs
to be done either within the hour or within

01:06:56.480 --> 01:07:02.690
the day. Whereas elective diagnostics coming
from outpatients or from GPs, need to be done

01:07:02.690 --> 01:07:08.010
within a matter of days or a week or two.
So, separating those two, but then the next

01:07:08.010 --> 01:07:12.330
question is, if you are then having to build
up the elective capacity, where would you

01:07:12.330 --> 01:07:18.540
do that, and the recommendation is that we
would do that away from acute sites in general.

01:07:18.540 --> 01:07:23.720
That will promote convenience for patients,
I think if it’s done well, it could contribute

01:07:23.720 --> 01:07:30.390
a net zero as well. I think it will undoubtedly
improve efficiency in terms of three report,

01:07:30.390 --> 01:07:35.290
if you are not having to mix the acute and
the elective patients all the time. It will

01:07:35.290 --> 01:07:42.130
be easier to keep things Covid free. And incidentally,
it is less expensive to build on a non-acute

01:07:42.130 --> 01:07:49.640
site than on an acute site.
And so, all of this is what we are referring

01:07:49.640 --> 01:07:55.930
to as community diagnostic hubs. Those hubs
need to be linked, of course, both to hospitals

01:07:55.930 --> 01:08:03.760
and to primary care, so the IT elements of
this are vital. And we need to get, whether

01:08:03.760 --> 01:08:08.660
it’s imaging people or pathology people
or endoscopy people, et cetera, working together

01:08:08.660 --> 01:08:14.080
in networks, which could very easily be at
the level of an ICS.

01:08:14.080 --> 01:08:19.359
Of course, we also need to look at new diagnostic
technologies. AI is coming. We’re probably

01:08:19.359 --> 01:08:27.520
going to see at first when it comes to mammography
and mammographic screening. I don’t think

01:08:27.520 --> 01:08:32.529
it’s immediately going to get rid of the
need for radiologists, for example. I do think

01:08:32.529 --> 01:08:38.731
over time they will be doing relatively more
invasive, interventional radiology, and less

01:08:38.731 --> 01:08:43.609
time probably spent in looking at X-rays.
We also need to look at point of care testing

01:08:43.609 --> 01:08:47.850
and wearables. But those will all come, so
we need to prepare for those.

01:08:47.850 --> 01:08:53.989
To drive the changes that I’m recommending,
it’s going to need leadership. I am delighted

01:08:53.989 --> 01:09:01.210
that NHS England and Improvement have already
set up a diagnostics programme board, with

01:09:01.210 --> 01:09:07.950
Steve Powis and Hugh McCaughey co-chairing
that. We’ve also got a workforce board in

01:09:07.950 --> 01:09:14.150
operation that had its first meeting this
afternoon, and we will need regional involvement

01:09:14.150 --> 01:09:19.580
and local involvement as well. And that’s
already beginning to happen, because diagnostics

01:09:19.580 --> 01:09:23.730
have frankly come to the top of the agenda
as a result of Covid.

01:09:23.730 --> 01:09:30.100
So, the benefits of all this. First and foremost,
there will be improved outcomes for patients,

01:09:30.100 --> 01:09:35.060
and it will make a big contribution toward
achieving long-term plan commitments such

01:09:35.060 --> 01:09:45.819
as the commitment to diagnose 75% of cancer
patients at stage I and II by 2028. So, benefits

01:09:45.819 --> 01:09:49.489
for patients.
Benefits in doing this in a planned way, if

01:09:49.489 --> 01:09:55.310
we bulk buy the equipment we need, we will
get substantial reductions. We’ve seen that

01:09:55.310 --> 01:10:02.580
in the past. By getting the imaging done in
a sensible way across networks, we will avoid

01:10:02.580 --> 01:10:08.130
duplication of imaging. We will reduce the
outsourcing which is costing is about £160

01:10:08.130 --> 01:10:13.840
million a year at the moment. There will be
the efficiencies of streamlining. We can really

01:10:13.840 --> 01:10:19.790
drive skill mix initiatives. And in the acute
sector, we can promote same-day emergency

01:10:19.790 --> 01:10:26.940
care by getting the scans done more quickly.
And in terms of impatience, there is really

01:10:26.940 --> 01:10:34.670
considerable potential to reduce length of
stay. We know that a lot of CT scans and MRIs

01:10:34.670 --> 01:10:40.390
are not done on the day that they are requested
as inpatients. They may not even be done the

01:10:40.390 --> 01:10:46.930
following day. And all of that leads to longer
lengths of stay. So, all of that, I believe

01:10:46.930 --> 01:10:56.200
there is potential to really improve outcomes
and have efficiencies to. I’ll stop there.

01:10:56.200 --> 01:11:02.680
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Mike.
Steve, did you want to comment on that at

01:11:02.680 --> 01:11:06.540
all? Steve Powis?
&gt;&gt;Stephen Powis: Just briefly, as Mike has

01:11:06.540 --> 01:11:13.630
said, and as the paper says, Hugh McCaughey
and myself, Hugh is not here I think, co-chair

01:11:13.630 --> 01:11:18.060
diagnostic board within NHS England and NHS
Improvement. We’ve been working closely

01:11:18.060 --> 01:11:27.000
with Mike as the report has taken shape, and
have considered the report in several meetings,

01:11:27.000 --> 01:11:32.750
and I just want to confirm that we are very,
very supportive of the work that Chris has

01:11:32.750 --> 01:11:39.520
done – that Mike has done, and the report.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you, Steve. Dido.

01:11:39.520 --> 01:11:45.040
&gt;&gt;Dido Harding: Mike, thank you for a really
excellent piece of work. It’s fantastic

01:11:45.040 --> 01:11:51.090
and really exciting. Just a question. I wondered
if you would talk a bit more about the digital

01:11:51.090 --> 01:11:57.650
element in the report, and how you see this
as a means of making a big step forward in

01:11:57.650 --> 01:12:02.180
patients having ready and easy access to all
their own test results and making those more

01:12:02.180 --> 01:12:05.790
portable and easier for us as citizens to
use?

01:12:05.790 --> 01:12:15.310
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: Thank you, Dido. The plan
to digitise all of this was actually in an

01:12:15.310 --> 01:12:22.130
imaging policy and the pathology policy already.
It just hasn’t had the capital that is needed

01:12:22.130 --> 01:12:29.790
to make it happen. And I think we now need
to drive that. I think that will enable hospitals

01:12:29.790 --> 01:12:33.920
to communicate with each other, community
diagnostic hubs to communicate both with hospitals

01:12:33.920 --> 01:12:40.070
and with primary care, and absolutely no reason
why that can’t also include – including

01:12:40.070 --> 01:12:45.610
patients themselves having access to all of
their own information. So, that, to me, is

01:12:45.610 --> 01:12:51.051
a critical element of all this.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: So, Mike, if you were to sort

01:12:51.051 --> 01:12:59.470
of gaze into the future, a genomic future
with point of care saliva testing and wearables

01:12:59.470 --> 01:13:05.850
and sensors and everything else, are we going
to need doctors at all, do you think?

01:13:05.850 --> 01:13:13.070
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: Yes, David. We are. I don’t
know if I need to say more than that. I think

01:13:13.070 --> 01:13:19.380
the human element will be there. Of course
technology is going to assist us. It’s assisting

01:13:19.380 --> 01:13:26.360
us all the time now. But I don’t think – I
wouldn’t be worried for members of my own

01:13:26.360 --> 01:13:28.880
profession that they’re going to be out
of a job for a very long time to come.

01:13:28.880 --> 01:13:33.390
&gt;&gt;David Prior: I thought you’d probably
say that. I happen to agree with you as well.

01:13:33.390 --> 01:13:36.250
Ruth?
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you very much, Mike. Nice

01:13:36.250 --> 01:13:41.820
to see you again. Great report, and good to
see the focus on this particular area of care

01:13:41.820 --> 01:13:50.990
for all of our patients. Your recommendations
14 and 15 around needing additional staff.

01:13:50.990 --> 01:13:59.400
I think for me it’s about how people work
as a team. How we have all sorts of people

01:13:59.400 --> 01:14:04.610
from different backgrounds opening our eyes
about how each one of us can work very differently

01:14:04.610 --> 01:14:08.980
to be in a team. And I think that’s the
big thing that I personally learned through

01:14:08.980 --> 01:14:16.630
the Covid is actually we’ve widened the
team out from all sorts of different specialities

01:14:16.630 --> 01:14:21.820
to care for our patients, and I’m hoping
that through your work now that we can further

01:14:21.820 --> 01:14:26.420
build on that so that we are looking much
more into the team approach rather than just

01:14:26.420 --> 01:14:31.840
one professional or another.
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: Ruth, I completely agree

01:14:31.840 --> 01:14:37.290
with that, and I think we do need to learn
from the experience with Covid and the things

01:14:37.290 --> 01:14:41.260
that have happened really very quickly as
a result of Covid, let’s make sure that

01:14:41.260 --> 01:14:49.940
that now happens and is carried forward with
diagnostics. Yes, I think teamwork. If you

01:14:49.940 --> 01:14:56.320
take imaging, we need to be looking at a skills
escalator, bringing in people who may be out

01:14:56.320 --> 01:15:03.250
of jobs from other sectors immediately who
can come in and they can then go up the skills

01:15:03.250 --> 01:15:09.820
ladder over time. I think we really need to
push the skill mix agenda. We’ve pushed

01:15:09.820 --> 01:15:16.440
it in certain areas of diagnostics. 15 years
ago, we pushed it in breast screening by bringing

01:15:16.440 --> 01:15:22.360
in assistant practitioners. That took an awful
lot of hard work to get that to happen. I

01:15:22.360 --> 01:15:28.770
know, to my certain cost, but it was possible.
But we haven’t extended that to other areas

01:15:28.770 --> 01:15:35.610
of diagnostics. We now need to do that. I
think in endoscopy, if we have good working

01:15:35.610 --> 01:15:42.810
between endoscopy nurses as well as the endoscopists
themselves, and of course some of the endoscopists

01:15:42.810 --> 01:15:48.350
are nurses, that can make a difference as
well. And we have that in the screening programme

01:15:48.350 --> 01:15:53.680
but we don’t do it in symptomatic colonoscopies.
Don’t ask me why. So, you know, there’s

01:15:53.680 --> 01:16:00.290
a lot we can learn.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Mike. Tim. Tim Ferris.

01:16:00.290 --> 01:16:08.720
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: Thank you, David. Professor
Richards, your paper was terrific, and congratulations

01:16:08.720 --> 01:16:18.200
to the team. I’d like to just – if I were
to reflect on how I would approach doing something

01:16:18.200 --> 01:16:26.320
that is absolutely necessary and much better
patient care, something that as you are laying

01:16:26.320 --> 01:16:32.810
out here in your paper in the United States,
I expect I would encounter an extraordinary

01:16:32.810 --> 01:16:39.900
amount of resistance from the professional
societies. And I wonder if you might comment

01:16:39.900 --> 01:16:52.700
on sort of the downfield blocking and challenges
that your plan will face here in England?

01:16:52.700 --> 01:16:57.890
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: Tim, I think there are some
really interesting comparisons internationally.

01:16:57.890 --> 01:17:02.380
So, for example, with our community diagnostic
hubs that we are recommending, and awful lot

01:17:02.380 --> 01:17:07.290
of other countries do diagnostics much closer
to home than we do. They don’t do it all

01:17:07.290 --> 01:17:14.140
on acute hospital sites. In terms of the professions,
I think sometimes there may be advantages

01:17:14.140 --> 01:17:19.990
in being in a very acute situation where we
desperately need more staff. We are not going

01:17:19.990 --> 01:17:24.350
to get more doctors to do everything like
this.

01:17:24.350 --> 01:17:30.250
And, you know, I’m an oncologist by background.
I remember the days when it was doctors that

01:17:30.250 --> 01:17:41.690
gave the chemotherapy. And now it is taken
for granted that that is not done by doctors.

01:17:41.690 --> 01:17:47.700
So, I’m really pleased that we can push
that. I think we’ve shown it with nurse

01:17:47.700 --> 01:17:54.890
endoscopists already. A lot of our flexible
sigmoidoscopy, a lot of upper GI endoscopy

01:17:54.890 --> 01:18:01.350
is already done by non-medical staff. So,
we can be leaders in the world in that. And

01:18:01.350 --> 01:18:05.420
yes, there might be more resistance in the
United States than there is here.

01:18:05.420 --> 01:18:10.300
&gt;&gt;Tim Ferris: Great. Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Ian.

01:18:10.300 --> 01:18:17.570
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: So, just a primary care perspective,
Mike, I think your report will be hugely welcomed.

01:18:17.570 --> 01:18:23.580
There’s been massive pent-up appetite for
decades the primary care to be able to access

01:18:23.580 --> 01:18:30.450
a larger array of diagnostics in the community,
and there will be an enormous appetite both

01:18:30.450 --> 01:18:36.710
for primary care networks and community providers
working really closely together as part of

01:18:36.710 --> 01:18:43.670
our integrated out-of-hospital delivery systems
to help be part of the solution in delivering

01:18:43.670 --> 01:18:48.130
the community diagnostic hubs.
And then just connecting this back to the

01:18:48.130 --> 01:18:54.680
conversation we were having around estate,
as we think not just about the future hospital

01:18:54.680 --> 01:18:59.080
build programme, but actually the modernisation
of the primary care estate, I know Emily’s

01:18:59.080 --> 01:19:04.570
team have been doing fantastic work on what
the blueprints look like for new integrated

01:19:04.570 --> 01:19:10.280
primary and community facilities, of which
diagnostics will be an important component

01:19:10.280 --> 01:19:17.650
of different tiered levels.
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: Ian, absolutely, and we have

01:19:17.650 --> 01:19:22.400
been talking closely with primary care colleagues
throughout this. It reminds me to say one

01:19:22.400 --> 01:19:28.460
thing, which is about phlebotomy services,
which often get overlooked. In some parts

01:19:28.460 --> 01:19:33.050
of the country, you can get phlebotomy done
in primary care six days a week. In my own

01:19:33.050 --> 01:19:37.210
practice, I can get it done half a morning
a week, and otherwise they expect me to go

01:19:37.210 --> 01:19:41.900
to an acute hospital, which is not the right
thing to be doing at this stage. We need to

01:19:41.900 --> 01:19:47.420
improve our phlebotomy services. And the more
we move outpatient work out of hospitals,

01:19:47.420 --> 01:19:50.840
the patients are still going to need blood
tests, so there will be more work to be done

01:19:50.840 --> 01:19:55.130
in the community. So, I would ask people to
really take an interest in that particular

01:19:55.130 --> 01:19:56.500
aspect.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, Mike.

01:19:56.500 --> 01:20:00.960
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Mike, can I just coming briefly
on that just to say I couldn’t agree more.

01:20:00.960 --> 01:20:08.270
Again, Covid in particular has emphasised
the weakness of the fact we’ve never had

01:20:08.270 --> 01:20:13.710
a comprehensive phlebotomy service in primary
care, and it’s really striking how, as you

01:20:13.710 --> 01:20:17.620
say, in some parts of the country, e.g. if
you look at Tower Hamlets, you have a far

01:20:17.620 --> 01:20:21.290
bigger volume of phlebotomy that is actually
happening in the community already, and I

01:20:21.290 --> 01:20:25.420
think the trick is, how do we use your report
as well as activity already underway linked

01:20:25.420 --> 01:20:31.120
to Covid to try and achieve that goal.
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: And I’ve talked to Sam

01:20:31.120 --> 01:20:35.480
Everington, too.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Right. Patrick.

01:20:35.480 --> 01:20:43.360
&gt;&gt;Patrick Carter: Thank you, David. Mike,
it’s a wonderfully clear and logical report.

01:20:43.360 --> 01:20:47.850
Thank you. It was a delight to go through.
I wondered, just a couple of points you might

01:20:47.850 --> 01:20:55.250
expand on. The first was AI, the Kheiron sort
of collaboration. Could you give some sense

01:20:55.250 --> 01:21:03.450
of the speed at which AI will impact both
radiology and pathology, particularly in histopathology,

01:21:03.450 --> 01:21:11.340
something you’ve picked up on?
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: Thank you very much for that,

01:21:11.340 --> 01:21:16.720
Patrick. I think it is moving. It’s moving
in imaging, it’s moving in pathology, it’s

01:21:16.720 --> 01:21:24.500
moving in endoscopy, too. I think, as I said,
mammography is almost certainly going to be

01:21:24.500 --> 01:21:29.520
the first, partly because in breast screening
where we currently have two humans looking

01:21:29.520 --> 01:21:36.170
at every single image, and I think where we
will move first is to replace one of the human

01:21:36.170 --> 01:21:41.710
readers with the machine. But it’s got to
be done well, and it’s being tested, and

01:21:41.710 --> 01:21:46.739
there are big large-scale studies of that
going on in the East Midlands, now, for example.

01:21:46.739 --> 01:21:51.291
And with others, as well.
I think chest x-rays may come next, but they

01:21:51.291 --> 01:21:56.750
are complicated, because a chest x-ray does
not just have long. It has rates, it has heart,

01:21:56.750 --> 01:22:02.460
it has all sorts in it, and the same is true
with CT scans of the chest, which extends

01:22:02.460 --> 01:22:07.840
down and cover part of the liver and the spleen
and goodness knows what. So, they are – they

01:22:07.840 --> 01:22:15.820
are coming. They will probably be most useful
for try arching. This one is very, very low

01:22:15.820 --> 01:22:22.270
risk, this is very high risk, can it be looked
at by a radiologist most quickly? The real

01:22:22.270 --> 01:22:26.490
point where this gets interesting is, can
we say this is so low-risk that it doesn’t

01:22:26.490 --> 01:22:33.370
have to be reported by human? I think with
histopathology, the first step, Patrick, is

01:22:33.370 --> 01:22:38.850
we need to digitise histopathology.
You know, if we want to be able to do the

01:22:38.850 --> 01:22:43.780
AI, we’ve got to have this not just on a
glass slide. We’ve got to have it properly

01:22:43.780 --> 01:22:53.310
digitised. And I think then it will come in
first probably for small biopsy samples, polyps

01:22:53.310 --> 01:22:58.390
taken out of the bowel and bowel screening,
or prostate samples, et cetera. I certainly

01:22:58.390 --> 01:23:04.830
think it will come in there, but let’s get
it digitised first. And I think in endoscopy,

01:23:04.830 --> 01:23:10.350
it’s exciting, but what is exciting about
there is it may improve the accuracy of endoscopy

01:23:10.350 --> 01:23:15.570
not necessary the speed.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks. Thank you, Mike. Amanda,

01:23:15.570 --> 01:23:22.550
I mean from all your experience of running
big acute integrated hot and cold hospitals,

01:23:22.550 --> 01:23:31.120
how do you see community diagnostics?
&gt;&gt;Amanda Pritchard: Thank you. So I was debating

01:23:31.120 --> 01:23:36.220
whether to come in or not without just sounding
sycophantic, because I do think that this

01:23:36.220 --> 01:23:42.719
is a really super piece of work, and particularly
because what it does is recognise the importance

01:23:42.719 --> 01:23:49.510
of seeing diagnostics as a whole rather than
just individual specialties, and individual

01:23:49.510 --> 01:23:53.020
modalities.
So, I think it’s – and, as other colleagues

01:23:53.020 --> 01:23:58.021
have said, actually one of my very first jobs
was as general manager looking after diagnostic

01:23:58.021 --> 01:24:01.980
services and in fact outpatient, so it’s
just great to see some of those services that

01:24:01.980 --> 01:24:08.330
sometimes don’t get the attention absolutely
elevated to giving them sort of, again, right

01:24:08.330 --> 01:24:14.480
at the top of the national radar in the way
that I think they now have and they need to.

01:24:14.480 --> 01:24:18.720
So, certainly, I mean, I’ve been involved
in setting up community diagnostic type hubs

01:24:18.720 --> 01:24:23.160
before as part of different models of care
over the last sort of 20 years, and I think

01:24:23.160 --> 01:24:27.680
where they work well, and particularly now
that we are in a sort of with Covid world,

01:24:27.680 --> 01:24:34.670
you can see that they are going to be I think
phenomenally effective at providing really

01:24:34.670 --> 01:24:39.680
efficient, really patient centred, really
easily accessible services. And if we can

01:24:39.680 --> 01:24:44.600
get the wraparound on the workforce, which
colleagues have mentioned, on the digital

01:24:44.600 --> 01:24:50.900
AI side and the physical location to come
together, then certainly, from my perspective,

01:24:50.900 --> 01:24:55.730
I can see why this has been so widely welcomed,
not just by Royal Colleges, but by all of

01:24:55.730 --> 01:24:59.570
those people who’s been involved in this
work so far, and I’m extremely keen to support

01:24:59.570 --> 01:25:05.150
us to take this forward.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: David Behan, did you want to

01:25:05.150 --> 01:25:10.960
raise any issues around workforce issues?
No, David –

01:25:10.960 --> 01:25:20.060
&gt;&gt;David Behan: Yeah, no, I’m with you. Thanks.
No, I think this is, like others have said,

01:25:20.060 --> 01:25:26.790
a hugely important report, and knowing Mike
as I do, it’s not surprising. I think what

01:25:26.790 --> 01:25:32.489
it is challenging me to think about though
is both – it’s articulating the demand

01:25:32.489 --> 01:25:37.690
for staff in diagnostics, and I think a lot
of thought now needs to be given to the supply

01:25:37.690 --> 01:25:42.900
of staff. Where are they going to come from
and whether the training pipeline is factored

01:25:42.900 --> 01:25:49.870
in. And I think that is work still to be done.
So, I think the diagnostics board has some

01:25:49.870 --> 01:25:55.489
important work to do to actually begin to
give action to some of the recommendations

01:25:55.489 --> 01:26:01.460
that Mike has made. But hugely important.
I’d personally echo the issues around skill

01:26:01.460 --> 01:26:08.310
mix, around the use of technology to support
the delivery of these recommendations, and

01:26:08.310 --> 01:26:16.990
that’s where some of the conversations elsewhere
on our agenda talked about the transformation

01:26:16.990 --> 01:26:22.390
agenda, and I think the report of itself,
using some of those technologies, digital,

01:26:22.390 --> 01:26:26.760
AI, et cetera, can be transformative in the
way that we provide these services. But the

01:26:26.760 --> 01:26:35.370
key issue for me, David is to just think through
what the workforce suppliers. If this is articulating

01:26:35.370 --> 01:26:42.000
the demand, what is the workforce supply that
we can begin to grow over this next period

01:26:42.000 --> 01:26:48.780
of time? Thank you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks, David. Noel Gordon.

01:26:48.780 --> 01:26:57.710
Sorry, Noel, I didn’t see you. Sorry.
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Thanks, David. This is a really

01:26:57.710 --> 01:27:04.290
practical report, Mike, and it’s practical
in the sense that many of the building blocks

01:27:04.290 --> 01:27:11.550
are more or less in place, and I’m particularly
thinking of the technology infrastructure

01:27:11.550 --> 01:27:19.770
around messaging, imaging tools, patient records.
I mean, a lot of that is there waiting to

01:27:19.770 --> 01:27:26.050
be leveraged. And you alluded to pilot sites,
and I’m thinking of EMRAD in the East Midlands,

01:27:26.050 --> 01:27:32.980
which of course is one of our pioneering sites
for centralised hubs. My question is more

01:27:32.980 --> 01:27:40.820
around what can we get on with in the short
term, given how important the renewal element

01:27:40.820 --> 01:27:48.040
is and the recovery element post Covid, as
it were? And what do you think we can start

01:27:48.040 --> 01:27:54.590
doing in the next six months that would get
us some way toward your – your vision, given

01:27:54.590 --> 01:28:05.360
that a lot of it might require capital funding?
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: Noel, thank you. I think

01:28:05.360 --> 01:28:12.180
the simple answer is that the NHS is getting
on with this. We have been feeding elements

01:28:12.180 --> 01:28:19.360
of this report through to the regions and
subregions for some weeks if not months now

01:28:19.360 --> 01:28:25.420
in response to Covid. I think that’s why
I’m so sure that there is a positive response

01:28:25.420 --> 01:28:30.050
here. Regional colleagues and others have
been coming back to us saying, let’s move

01:28:30.050 --> 01:28:38.530
as fast as we possibly can. We have a diagnostics
recovery implementation group that is actually

01:28:38.530 --> 01:28:44.700
driving this.
I think we can only go so far. We have what’s

01:28:44.700 --> 01:28:51.960
called an adopt and adapt programme, which
is leading on this, but we do need the capital

01:28:51.960 --> 01:28:58.870
in the first place to start getting some of
these community diagnostic hubs up and running.

01:28:58.870 --> 01:29:05.140
The NHS is really wanting to drive this and
is going as fast as it can, but, yes, it will

01:29:05.140 --> 01:29:13.530
need the capital to get the community diagnostic
hubs and the IT infrastructure in place. Those

01:29:13.530 --> 01:29:17.430
are the two most important things.
I think the workforce, which David Behan was

01:29:17.430 --> 01:29:22.300
mentioning, is hugely important, and in the
longer term, probably the most difficult.

01:29:22.300 --> 01:29:29.120
I think we can do a lot on skill mix quite
quickly if we really want to get it done,

01:29:29.120 --> 01:29:38.870
and I’m delighted that the joint HEE NHS
E&amp;I board is now in action, and I impressed

01:29:38.870 --> 01:29:43.860
on them earlier this afternoon the urgency
of this. But we also need to be looking at

01:29:43.860 --> 01:29:50.180
bringing new people in at the bottom end of
the escalator ladder. So, I think there is

01:29:50.180 --> 01:29:57.300
a lot we can do, but we will need more capital.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Julian. Julian, I think you

01:29:57.300 --> 01:30:03.150
had your hand up.
&gt;&gt;Julian Kelly: I was just going to say that

01:30:03.150 --> 01:30:09.110
I think, I mean, will have to do this through
the diagnostic programme board. It’s developing

01:30:09.110 --> 01:30:16.010
[inaudible] how we do also the funding model
with systems, and really getting that by in,

01:30:16.010 --> 01:30:21.180
and I think, you know, even if there is national
funding, how do we make the engagement work

01:30:21.180 --> 01:30:26.120
with the kind of right join up of funding
from a kind of local and national to really

01:30:26.120 --> 01:30:33.080
make this fly and get the ownership, and I
think that’s one of the key things we now

01:30:33.080 --> 01:30:37.489
need to work on. And I think that will be
critical as we go through the spending round.

01:30:37.489 --> 01:30:44.500
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thanks very much, Julian. Mike,
at the risk of joining Amanda in being sycophantic

01:30:44.500 --> 01:30:48.501
as well, just on behalf of us all, thank you
very much. It’s a terrific report. Thank

01:30:48.501 --> 01:30:50.070
you.
&gt;&gt;Mike Richards: My pleasure.

01:30:50.070 --> 01:30:55.340
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Good. So the next item is the
public participation dashboard. Ruth.

01:30:55.340 --> 01:31:03.460
&gt;&gt;Ruth May: Thank you very much, chair. It
is the starred item for note. It is the work

01:31:03.460 --> 01:31:11.320
of Olivia and Neal and their teams. We know,
and quite rightly we need to consult. We have

01:31:11.320 --> 01:31:17.570
a duty to do so. Equally, we know the value
of the patient and the public voice, and I

01:31:17.570 --> 01:31:23.700
just wanted to commend this report to you.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Thank you very much, Ruth.

01:31:23.700 --> 01:31:34.700
Have we got any other business? Simon, have
you got anything you wanted to end up with?

01:31:34.700 --> 01:31:40.050
No.
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: No, I think that’s the

01:31:40.050 --> 01:31:44.120
business we had before us. Thank you, David.
&gt;&gt;David Prior: Okay. Thank you all very much.

